 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>At Ogata, where Mame Kurogouchi presents her collections with the serene formality of a Japanese tea ceremony, the subject was something considerably more democratic: the school uniform. In Japan, where the uniform is less a dress code than a formative part of adolescence, Kurogouchi looked at what happens when everybody is given essentially the same clothes, and then proceeds to make them their own.</p><p>Her starting point was not nostalgia so much as memory: the family albums, the anonymous details accumulated through generations of school dress; the particular ways a skirt is worn, a tie knotted, a stand collar adjusted, a jacket made subtly one’s own. Those small acts of deviation became the collection’s real subject. The uniform may prescribe the silhouette; the wearer supplies the personality.</p><p>Kurogouchi translated the codes of blazers, gakuran jackets, sailor collars, checks, and pleats into a wardrobe that was elongated, soft, and gracefully exacting. Tailoring was stretched into serene proportions, its severity softened by curved lines and sheer materials. A blazer and skirt merged into a single pleated minidress; sailor collars migrated onto MA-1 jackets, blouses, and knitwear; stripes shifted into checks, while multiple checks were twisted and shirted, made to undulate through shibori techniques.</p><p>Hand-embroidery provided another layer of memory. Flowers and plants, drawn from traditional kimono motifs, appeared almost like fragments of another wardrobe, while brooches suggested school emblems without becoming literal. Against the disciplined palette of black, navy, and white, colored socks supplied a small flash of adolescent disobedience. It was a reminder that even the strictest uniform tends to acquire a rather rebellious life of its own once it meets a teenager.</p><p>There was something characteristically Mame Kurogouchi about the way severity was allowed to remain severity. She didn’t turn the school uniform into a costume or sentimentalize it into Japanese nostalgia. Instead, she softened its authority, stretching its proportions and letting embroidery, transparency, and movement do the talking. It was an elegant, slightly austere collection, and all the more graceful for resisting prettiness.</p></div>",
